[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-19", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 19 — the ordered drive: every read at the wire; Datalog 256 / 1022 / 1533 ops/s by slot column", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-19.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 19 - the ordered drive: every read at the wire, and the Datalog path passes its index twin\n\n> Seed: iteration 18's S6 - an ordered walk of creationDate so IC02/IC09\n> stop at 20 instead of materialising friend-of-friend x messages and\n> pruning afterwards. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2, rung SF1 subset,\n> origin :cross-host (simeon), mix IC02/07/08/09, impl :datalog\n> (reference 190.2 ops/s at 8 slots, iteration 18).\n\n## Generate\n\n  The engine's own docstring had already named the correct shape when the\n  first limit pushdown was reverted (arrangement a98588c): evaluate the\n  driver-independent clauses once, run only the dependent ones per value\n  group, semi-join the two. Written as `ordered-drive` in datalog.core\n  (77197339): for :order-by led by a variable bound in value position by\n  exactly one clause (or its fused range) plus :limit, walk that\n  attribute's distinct values in order (a per-(attribute, db version)\n  sorted vector, cached by identity of the :avet inner map), run the\n  clauses that mention the driver's variables per chunk of values, every\n  other clause once, hash semi-join on shared variables, stop at the\n  limit. The plain join is untouched and is the reference.\n\n  Prediction: IC09 empty answers -> a few ms; IC09 served -> the rtt; the\n  Datalog row at 8 slots -> the rtt ceiling (~250, level with the index\n  column).\n\n## Rank - in-process first (official parameters, simeon)\n\n    ic09 empty   63.1 -> 2.7 ms     ic02 empty 0.8 -> 0.1\n    ic02 nonempty 1.1 -> 17.1 ms    <- the unbounded drive LOST here\n\n  Twenty hits among 30 friends' messages are 0.3% of 86,932 dates: the\n  drive walked ~6,000 values. So the walk got a budget (512 values, then\n  the plain join takes over): ic02 nonempty 2.4, ic09 empty 3.1. Budget\n  128 was tried: ic02 1.8 but the dense IC09 (highest-degree persons) no\n  longer filled 20 within it and fell back (63.5 vs 41.7 ms). 512 kept.\n  The dense IC09 stays at ~41 ms with or without chunking the walk: its\n  cost is the INDEPENDENT half, the two-hop friend-of-friend join with\n  ~30k intermediate bindings -- the next thing, not this one.\n\n  Tests: drive vs plain join on IC02/IC08/IC09 shapes, ties, empty\n  ranges, an unknown person, both directions (49 assertions; a\n  wrong-direction drive fails 18); an early-stop test counts visible?\n  calls. datalog 126/278/0; kotobase-peer on the new floor 261/798/0.\n\n## Rank - served (cross-host, 392/392 agreement with the index twins)\n\n    impl     slots  rate  on-time%  thru/s   ic02  ic07  ic08  ic09\n    datalog    8     256  100.00    255.9   11.8  11.9  12.4  14.5   <- 190 -> 256\n    index      8     256  100.00    255.2   10.9  10.2  10.0  50.4\n    datalog   32    1024  100.00   1022.4   12.7  12.9  13.3  15.6\n    datalog   32    1536   94.69   1472.7                              (0.31 under the rule)\n    index     32     512  100.00    493.9   42.8  42.2  42.3  94.7\n    datalog   64    1536  100.00   1533.2   15.2  15.2  15.6  18.4\n    datalog   64    2048   37.58   1797.4\n\n  A first 8-slot sweep had 256 failing at 56.6% on-time while the driver\n  host sat at load 44; re-run back to back with the index arm, both pass.\n  The driver host's load (27-45, other agents) can only make a row worse.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Prediction held: IC09 at the rtt, the 8-slot row at the wire ceiling.\n    Beyond 8 slots the Datalog path now passes its index twin, whose\n    IC09 still materialises friend-of-friend messages: 1022 vs 494 at 32\n    slots. Ratio to the audited No.1: 545 / 136 / 91 by slot column, raw.\n  - The drive is a trade with a budget, and both sides of the trade were\n    measured before the budget was chosen. The engine cannot know the hit\n    density without statistics; 512 values is where the sparse case pays\n    under 2 ms and the dense case fills.\n  - Two engine features this loop touched were each correct and slow in\n    the case they were built for (the bound-entity range, the value walk\n    that re-derived everything). Both were found by reading the branch\n    and then measured; the second was rewritten in the shape its own\n    docstring had asked for.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH on the Datalog column at 8\nslots (190.2 -> 255.9); new 32- and 64-slot rows (1022 / 1533). Index\ncolumn unchanged.\n\nSeeds for iteration 20, in order:\n\n  - S9 (independent half): the dense IC09's 41 ms is the two-hop\n    friend-of-friend join carrying ~30k bindings; the index twin does the\n    same in ~1 ms with sets. A join step whose output is projected to one\n    variable could be computed as a set from the start (semi-join\n    pruning before materialising bindings). Predicted: dense IC09 -> ~5 ms.\n  - S2 (rung): full SF1 on simeon. The 35k-subset db is 1.3 GB of heap for\n    810k quads; full SF1 (~16.5M quads) does not fit 16 GiB at that rate,\n    so a rung above SF1-subset needs either a larger host or a smaller\n    per-quad footprint -- measure the footprint first.\n  - S3 (mix): short reads IS1-IS7.\n  - S10 (wire): every read is at the rtt; the next row on this axis is\n    a driver on the same LAN (rtt ~1 ms) -- a different origin column.\n"}]
